.he "jobctl (2)                    10/25/26                    jobctl (2)"
.fo "				    -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
jobctl
- control a process' scheduling and terminal input


.in -5
.bd
C
.in +5
.br
jobctl(pid, op)


.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Jobctl
sets or clears two switches the kernel keeps for the process with the
given ID.

.bd
JOBSTOP
takes the process off the scheduler; it is not run until
.bd
JOBCONT
clears the switch.  A stopped process is still alive: it may be
signalled, and it may be waited for.

.bd
JOBTTYOUT
closes the process' terminal input.  A read from the controlling
terminal returns end of file, and a read already asleep in the terminal
is woken and returns end of file without taking a line from the
foreground.
.bd
JOBTTYIN
opens it again.

The kernel knows nothing of jobs - not grouping, not job numbers, not
names.  These are the two things a shell cannot do for itself, and it
stops and resumes a pipeline by naming each of its processes.

The sending and receiving processes must have the same effective user
IDs, or the sender must be the super-user.

A process can never name itself.


.in -5
.bd
RETURNS
.in +5
.br
A 0 indicates success, a -1 indicates failure.


.in -5
.bd
ERRORS
.in +5
.br
.bd
ESRCH
if there is no such process, it has exited, or it is the caller.


.in -5
.bd
ASSEMBLER
.in +5
.br
.bd
(jobctl = 57)
.br
.bd
(process ID in hl)
.br
.bd
sys; jobctl; op


.in -5
.bd
RETURNS
.in +5
.br
The carry flag is cleared on success, or set on failure.
